{
  "schema_version": "1.7.0",
  "id": "USN-8916-2",
  "summary": "pyjwt vulnerabilities",
  "details": "USN-8916-1 fixed vulnerabilities in PyJWT. This update provides the\ncorresponding fix for PyJWT on Ubuntu 26.10.\n\nOriginal advisory details:\n\nYuSung Han discovered that PyJWT did not properly validate certain JWK keys\nwhen performing HMAC verification. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to forge authentication tokens. (CVE-2026-102266)\n\nRohan Das discovered that PyJWT incorrectly handled redirects when fetching\nkey sets. A remote attacker could possibly use this issue to  cause PyJWT\nto disclose sensitive information. (CVE-2026-102267)\n\nIt was discovered that PyJWT incorrectly recognized certain PEM\nrepresentations. An attacker who knew the public key could possibly use\nthis issue to forge authentication tokens. (CVE-2026-102268)\n\nNuno Lopes discovered that PyJWT incorrectly handled DER public keys when\nperforming HMAC verification. An attacker who knew the public key could\npossibly use this issue to forge authentication tokens. (CVE-2026-102271)\n\nIt was discovered that PyJWT did not properly handle certain JWK keys\nprefixed with a byte-order mark. An attacker who knew the public key could\npossibly use this issue to forge authentication tokens. (CVE-2026-102272)\n\nCharles Vosburgh discovered that PyJWT incorrectly handled certain JWK\ncontainers when performing HMAC verification. An attacker who knew the\npublic key could possibly use this issue to forge authentication tokens.\n(CVE-2026-102273)",
